\label{ch:effect-systems}

While type systems track what values a program can produce, \emph{effect systems} track what a program can \emph{do}---what side effects it can perform, what exceptions it can raise, what state it can modify. Effect systems make the computational behavior of programs explicit in their types, enabling reasoning about purity, resource usage, and concurrency. This chapter develops the theory of effect systems, from simple effect annotations to algebraic effects and handlers.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 50.1
%% ============================================================
\section{What Are Effects?}\label{sec:what-are-effects}

An \emph{effect} is any computational behavior beyond the production of a value. Common effects include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{State mutation.} Reading or writing mutable variables.
  \item \textbf{I/O.} Reading from or writing to files, networks, or the console.
  \item \textbf{Exceptions.} Raising or handling errors.
  \item \textbf{Non-determinism.} Making choices that affect the computation.
  \item \textbf{Concurrency.} Spawning threads, synchronizing, or communicating.
  \item \textbf{Allocation.} Allocating or freeing memory.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Effects as Type Information}\label{sec:effects-as-types}

An effect system extends the type judgment $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ with an effect annotation:
\[
  \Gamma \vdash e : \tau \mid \epsilon
\]
where $\epsilon$ describes the effects that $e$ may perform. A \emph{pure} expression has the empty effect $\epsilon = \emptyset$; an expression that may raise exceptions has $\epsilon \supseteq \{\mathrm{exn}\}$.

\subsection{Effect Lattices}\label{sec:effect-lattices}

Effects are typically organized as a lattice, where the join (least upper bound) represents the union of effects:

