\section{Effect Typing Rules}\label{sec:effect-typing}

Effect typing rules extend the standard typing rules with effect annotations.

\subsection{Basic Rules}\label{sec:basic-effect-rules}

\begin{semrules}{Effect Typing}
  \typeaxiom{Var}{\Gamma, x : \tau \vdash x : \tau \mid \emptyset} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs}{\Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e : \tau_2 \mid \epsilon}
            {\Gamma \vdash \lambda x.\, e : \tau_1 \xrightarrow{\epsilon} \tau_2 \mid \emptyset} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \xrightarrow{\epsilon_1} \tau_2 \mid \epsilon_1' \quad
                  \Gamma \vdash e_2 : \tau_1 \mid \epsilon_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e_1\; e_2 : \tau_2 \mid \epsilon_1 \cup \epsilon_1' \cup \epsilon_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Let}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \mid \epsilon_1 \quad
                  \Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e_2 : \tau_2 \mid \epsilon_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{let}\; x = e_1\; \mathtt{in}\; e_2 : \tau_2 \mid \epsilon_1 \cup \epsilon_2}
\end{semrules}

The key principle is \emph{effect union}: the effect of a composite expression is the union of the effects of its subexpressions.

\subsection{Effectful Operations}\label{sec:effectful-ops}

Specific operations have specific effects:

\begin{semrules}{Effectful Operations}
  \typeaxiom{Read}{\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{read}(r) : \tau \mid \{\mathrm{read}\}} \\[6pt]
  \typeaxiom{Write}{\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{write}(r, v) : \mathrm{unit} \mid \{\mathrm{write}\}} \\[6pt]
  \typeaxiom{Throw}{\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{throw}(e) : \tau \mid \{\mathrm{exn}\}} \\[6pt]
  \typeaxiom{Print}{\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{print}(s) : \mathrm{unit} \mid \{\mathrm{io}\}}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Effect Subtyping}\label{sec:effect-subtyping}

Effects are ordered by inclusion: if $\epsilon_1 \subseteq \epsilon_2$, then an expression with effect $\epsilon_1$ can be used where effect $\epsilon_2$ is expected (it does fewer effects).

\[
  \typerule{Effect-Sub}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau \mid \epsilon_1 \quad \epsilon_1 \subseteq \epsilon_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau \mid \epsilon_2}
\]

