\section{Effect Polymorphism}\label{sec:effect-polymorphism}

Effect polymorphism allows functions to be polymorphic in their effects: a higher-order function's effect depends on the effects of its function arguments.

\subsection{Effect Variables}\label{sec:effect-variables}

\begin{example}[Effect-polymorphic map]\label{ex:effect-map}
The function $\mathtt{map}$ has the effect-polymorphic type:
\[
  \mathtt{map} : \forall \epsilon.\, (\alpha \xrightarrow{\epsilon} \beta) \to [\alpha] \to [\beta] \mid \epsilon
\]
This says that $\mathtt{map}$ has exactly the effects of its function argument $f$. If $f$ is pure ($\epsilon = \emptyset$), then $\mathtt{map}$ is pure. If $f$ performs I/O, then $\mathtt{map}$ performs I/O.
\end{example}

\subsection{Effect Constraints}\label{sec:effect-constraints}

Effect polymorphism introduces effect constraints, analogous to type constraints in type-class systems:

\[
  \mathtt{compose} : \forall \epsilon_1, \epsilon_2.\, (\beta \xrightarrow{\epsilon_2} \gamma) \to (\alpha \xrightarrow{\epsilon_1} \beta) \to (\alpha \xrightarrow{\epsilon_1 \cup \epsilon_2} \gamma)
\]

\subsection{Effect Inference}\label{sec:effect-inference}

Effect inference extends type inference to determine the effects of expressions without explicit annotations. The algorithm is similar to HM type inference, but with effect variables and effect unification.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Effect Inference}\label{alg:effect-inference}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{InferEffect}{$\Gamma, e$}
  \State \textbf{case} $e$ \textbf{of}
  \State \quad $x$: \Return $\Gamma(x).\tau, \Gamma(x).\epsilon, \emptyset$
  \State \quad $\lambda x.\, e'$:
    \State \quad $\alpha \gets \mathrm{fresh}()$; $\epsilon \gets \mathrm{fresh\_effect}()$
    \State \quad $\tau', \epsilon', C \gets \mathrm{InferEffect}(\Gamma[x : \alpha \xrightarrow{\epsilon} \tau'], e')$
    \State \quad \Return $\alpha \xrightarrow{\epsilon} \tau', \emptyset, C$
  \State \quad $e_1\; e_2$:
    \State \quad $\tau_1 \xrightarrow{\epsilon_1} \tau_1', \epsilon_1', C_1 \gets \mathrm{InferEffect}(\Gamma, e_1)$
    \State \quad $\tau_2, \epsilon_2, C_2 \gets \mathrm{InferEffect}(\Gamma, e_2)$
    \State \quad $C_3 \gets \{\tau_2 = \tau_1\}$
    \State \quad \Return $\tau_1', \epsilon_1 \cup \epsilon_1' \cup \epsilon_2, C_1 \cup C_2 \cup C_3$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

