\section{Algebraic Effects and Handlers}\label{sec:algebraic-effects}

\emph{Algebraic effects}, introduced by Plotkin and Power~\cite{plotkin2003}, provide a modular framework for defining and composing effects. An effect is defined by a set of \emph{operations}; a \emph{handler} interprets those operations.

\subsection{Effect Signatures}\label{sec:effect-signatures}

\begin{definition}[Effect Signature]\label{def:effect-signature}
An \emph{effect signature} $\Sigma$ is a set of operation symbols, each with an arity specifying the types of its argument and the type of its continuation:
\[
  \Sigma = \{ \mathtt{op}_i : A_i \to B_i \}_{i \in I}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[State effect signature]\label{ex:state-signature}
The state effect with state type $S$ has two operations:
\begin{align*}
  \mathtt{get} &: \mathrm{unit} \to S \\
  \mathtt{set} &: S \to \mathrm{unit}
\end{align*}
\end{example}

\subsection{Effect Operations}\label{sec:effect-operations}

An effect operation is invoked by the program and handled by the handler:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Program using state effect
  let x = get() in
  set(x + 1);
  get()

  -- Handler interpreting state effect
  handle program with
    | get() -> k -> \s -> k(s)(s)
    | set(s') -> k -> \s -> k()(s')
    | return v -> \s -> (v, s)
\end{verbatim}

The handler receives a continuation $k$ for each operation, allowing it to decide how (and whether) to resume the computation.

\subsection{Handlers}\label{sec:handlers}

\begin{definition}[Handler]\label{def:handler}
A \emph{handler} for an effect signature $\Sigma$ consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A \emph{return clause} that handles the final value of the computation.
  \item An \emph{operation clause} for each $\mathtt{op}_i \in \Sigma$ that handles the operation, receiving the argument and a continuation.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Exception handler]\label{ex:exception-handler}
\begin{verbatim}
  handle
    let x = 1 / 0 in
    x + 1
  with
    | raise() -> k -> 0   -- ignore the exception, return 0
    | return v -> v
\end{verbatim}
The handler catches the division-by-zero exception and returns 0 instead.
\end{example}

\subsection{Multi-Shot Continuations}\label{sec:multi-shot}

A key feature of algebraic effects is that handlers can invoke the continuation \emph{multiple times} or \emph{zero times}:

\begin{example}[Non-determinism handler]\label{ex:nondeterminism}
\begin{verbatim}
  handle
    let x = choose([1, 2, 3]) in
    let y = choose([4, 5]) in
    (x, y)
  with
    | choose(xs) -> k -> concatMap k xs  -- run for each choice
    | return v -> [v]
\end{verbatim}
The handler invokes the continuation once for each element of the choice list, collecting all results. This implements backtracking search.
\end{example}

