\section{Effect Inference}\label{sec:effect-inference-adv}

Effect inference determines the effects of expressions without explicit annotations. The challenge is that effects, unlike types, form a lattice rather than a free algebra, making unification more complex.

\subsection{Effect Unification}\label{sec:effect-unification}

Effect unification solves equations of the form $\epsilon_1 = \epsilon_2$ where $\epsilon_i$ are effect expressions built from effect variables, effect constants, union ($\cup$), and intersection ($\cap$).

\begin{example}[Effect unification]\label{ex:effect-unification}
The equation $\epsilon \cup \{\mathrm{io}\} = \{\mathrm{io}, \mathrm{exn}\}$ has the solution $\epsilon = \{\mathrm{exn}\}$ (or any $\epsilon \subseteq \{\mathrm{exn}\}$).
\end{example}

\subsection{Effect Inference in Practice}\label{sec:effect-inference-practice}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Koka.} Microsoft Research's Koka language performs full effect inference, determining the effects of every expression without annotations.
  \item \textbf{Eff.} The Eff language uses algebraic effects with handlers, requiring explicit handler annotations but inferring effect types.
  \item \textbf{Frank.} Frank combines effect inference with session types, tracking both effects and communication protocols.
\end{itemize}

