\section{Comparison with Monadic Effects}\label{sec:monadic-effects}

The monadic approach to effects (used by Haskell) and the algebraic effects approach (used by Koka, Eff) represent two fundamentally different philosophies.

\subsection{Monadic Effects}\label{sec:monadic-approach}

In the monadic approach, each effect is represented by a monad:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathrm{State}\; S\; A$: stateful computation producing $A$.
  \item $\mathrm{IO}\; A$: I/O computation producing $A$.
  \item $\mathrm{Maybe}\; A$: computation that may fail.
\end{itemize}

Effects are composed using monad transformers: $\mathrm{StateT}\; S\; (\mathrm{ExceptT}\; E\; \mathrm{IO})\; A$ represents a computation with state, exceptions, and I/O.

\subsection{Algebraic Effects}\label{sec:algebraic-approach}

In the algebraic approach, effects are defined by operations and composed by union:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\{\mathrm{get} : \mathrm{unit} \to S, \mathrm{set} : S \to \mathrm{unit}\}$: state effect.
  \item $\{\mathrm{raise} : E \to \mathrm{void}\}$: exception effect.
  \item $\{\mathrm{read} : \mathrm{String} \to \mathrm{String}, \mathrm{write} : \mathrm{String} \to \mathrm{unit}\}$: I/O effect.
\end{itemize}

Effects are composed by simple union: $\{\mathrm{state}\} \cup \{\mathrm{exn}\} \cup \{\mathrm{io}\}$.

\subsection{Comparison}\label{sec:comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Monadic vs. algebraic effects}\label{tab:monadic-algebraic}
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Monadic} & \textbf{Algebraic} \\
\hline
Composition & Monad transformers & Effect union \\
Handler & \texttt{runState}, \texttt{runExcept} & \texttt{handle} with clauses \\
Ordering & Fixed by transformer stack & Flexible, any handler order \\
Extensibility & New transformer needed & New operation signature \\
Inference & Explicit monad type & Effect inference possible \\
Performance & Transformer overhead & Direct compilation \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{insight}
The algebraic effects approach is more modular than the monadic approach: effects are composed by union rather than by stacking, and handlers can be applied in any order. This eliminates the ``transformer hell'' of deeply nested monad transformers and enables more flexible effect handling. However, the monadic approach is more mature and has better tooling support in languages like Haskell.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
The idea of tracking effects in types dates back to the early 1990s, with effect systems for exceptions (Talpin and Jouvelot~\cite{talpin1992}) and state (Gifford and Lucassen~\cite{gifford1986}). The monadic approach, pioneered by Moggi~\cite{moggi1991} and popularized by Wadler~\cite{wadler1992}, became the dominant paradigm in Haskell. The algebraic effects approach, introduced by Plotkin and Power~\cite{plotkin2003} and developed by Plotkin and Pretnar~\cite{plotkin2013}, offers a more modular alternative. Modern languages like Koka, Unison, and Lean 4 have adopted algebraic effects, signaling a shift toward this more flexible approach.
\end{historical}

