\subsection{Effect-Based Optimization}\label{sec:effect-based-optimization}

Effect information enables powerful compiler optimizations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Purity-based parallelization.} Pure computations can be safely parallelized.
  \item \textbf{Effect-based inlining.} Pure functions can be inlined without concern for effect ordering.
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination.} Pure computations with unused results can be eliminated.
  \item \textbf{Memoization.} Pure functions can be memoized safely.
  \item \textbf{Effect specialization.} Generate specialized code for different effect profiles.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Effect-based parallelization]\label{ex:effect-parallel}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- If f is pure:
  results = par_map(f, data)  // safe to parallelize

  -- If f has IO:
  results = seq_map(f, data)  // must be sequential (IO ordering)

  -- The compiler selects the appropriate version based on effect analysis
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

